<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  Entitlements for Cmdr production builds (hardened runtime).
  Used during Apple code signing (codesign -\-entitlements).
  Required for notarization.

  Deliberately EMPTY. Every entitlement here is a hole in the hardened runtime, and
  none of the usual Tauri ones are load-bearing for us:

  - `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation` would let any library, signed
    by anyone, load into Cmdr. That is the standard way to inject code into a signed
    app, and nothing Cmdr loads needs it. The only `dlopen` is Apple's own
    `FileProvider.framework` (`file_system/file_provider_actions/objc.rs`), which
    library validation permits. The llama.cpp dylibs under `Resources/resources/ai/`
    are NOT a counterexample: `ai/extract.rs` copies them out and `llama-server` runs
    as a CHILD PROCESS, so they load into its address space and never into ours (and
    they carry our own Team ID anyway, which library validation permits).
  - `com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory` and `…allow-jit` are for
    a process that JITs. WKWebView's JavaScript runs in Apple's own out-of-process
    `com.apple.WebKit.WebContent`, not here.
  - `com.apple.security.automation.apple-events` is only needed to send an Apple Event
    from Cmdr's own process. Get Info's ask to Finder is the only event Cmdr sends another
    app, and it goes
    through a spawned `osascript`, which still gets the macOS prompt without it
    (`file_system/get_info.rs`). The prompt's reason is `NSAppleEventsUsageDescription`
    in `Info.plist`.

  ❌ Don't add one back without proving the app needs it on a SIGNED build: an
  unsigned dev build exercises none of this. If Cmdr ever does need library validation
  off, see `docs/specs/elevated-file-operations.md` decision 9 for what that costs.
-->
<dict>
</dict>
</plist>
